  "query_preview": "..After a break in precipitation the past few days,\nanother period of unsettled on potentially very wet weather is in\nstore starting early Friday. There is a brief lull in rain and\nthunderstorms through much of the day Saturday before the next\nwave of significant rainfall moves in later Saturday night through\nlate Sunday. The first shower and thunderstorms chances begin\nduring the early morning hours on Friday, as tropical moisture\nheads northward into our region and combines with an approaching\nupper level wave. A general 0.20\"-0.80\" rainfall is currently\nforecast from the Mogollon Rim/White Mtns southward, with some\nlocalized heavier amounts over 1\" for portions of southern Yavapai\nCounty and favored upslope regions along the rim. Some localized\nflooding could develop, again areas mainly south of the Mogollon\nRim would be favored.\n\nLate Friday evening through Saturday afternoon looks to be a lull\nin precipitation as the first wave moves eastward and the next\nstronger system begins to approach from the west. By Saturday\nnight through the day on Sunday, very deep moisture is forecast to\nmove across our region from the Pacific. Two upper level features\nwill also impact the region, one wave moving east off the Pacific\nat a fairly low latitude (starting at 30 degrees north), and the\nother is a deep upper low moving from the PacNW toward the Great\nBasin. The majority of guidance produces heavy rainfall across\nmuch of our forecast area, with the heaviest from the upslope\nregions of the Mogollon Rim southward, and somewhat lighter but\nstill significant rain to the north including Navajo Nation.\nCurrent projections are for widespread 1-3\" rainfall along/south\nof the Mogollon Rim, with locally higher amounts of 4\" in the most\nfavored upslope regions. With soils still wet from previous weeks\nof rain, this amount of additional rainfall would cause flooding \nissues. WPC has included all of our forecast area in a Slight Risk\nfor excessive rainfall late Saturday through Sunday, and we will\nbe closely monitoring this potential. Additionally, increased shear, \nlarge-scale ascent and colder air aloft raises the risk for severe\nweather, especially late Saturday night through Sunday.",
